WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.997
A connection may become visible only after a rule is expressed as its contrapositive.

2
00:00:06.297 --> 00:00:08.516
This changes the form, not the meaning.

3
00:00:08.816 --> 00:00:10.459
Every marked vial is sealed.

4
00:00:10.759 --> 00:00:12.721
Every open vial is unsealed.

5
00:00:13.021 --> 00:00:16.755
The first rule reaches sealed, while the second begins from open.

6
00:00:17.055 --> 00:00:20.319
Contrapose the second: a sealed vial is not open.

7
00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:25.077
Now the sealed result has somewhere to go, and marked vials cannot be open.

8
00:00:25.377 --> 00:00:30.391
The open-vial rule was not simply reversed; each side was negated as well.

9
00:00:30.691 --> 00:00:34.083
Negative wording can hide an otherwise direct connection.

10
00:00:34.383 --> 00:00:39.716
The useful question is whether an equivalent direction supplies the missing middle condition.

11
00:00:40.016 --> 00:00:45.008
Identify the condition already reached and compare it with both forms of the other rule.

12
00:00:45.308 --> 00:00:48.551
Keep existing negatives with the ideas they deny.

13
00:00:48.851 --> 00:00:52.563
Write a contrapositive when it helps you see or use a connection.

14
00:00:52.863 --> 00:01:00.969
Keep the original available while checking the transformation; once the chain is clear, one readable representation can do the work.
